well let's now speak to Abdullah  al-arian who's associate professor of  history at Georgetown University here in  Qatar and author of football in the  Middle East State society and the  Beautiful game Abdullah thank you very  much for being with us on Al Jazeera  such a life such a legacy how in your  view did Pele transform football  well I mean I think if we if we go back  to the era in which he emerged I mean in  the 1950s you didn't really have a  global uh athlete someone who kind of uh  you know embodied the kinds of qualities  that we tend to associate today with  some of the truly Global athletes and so  in some ways he was really the first  maybe alongside only someone like  Muhammad Ali someone who really captured  the imagination of populations across  the globe  um you know someone who who's who kind  of existed in a state of Mythology in a  way given the kinds of stories that  circulated around his performances long  before televisions were present in every  home and people would kind of have to  recap uh you know all of his  achievements winning a World Cup at such  a young age and then doing it again 12  years later having three of those  trophies something that has never been  equal let alone surpassed and unlikely  based on kind of what we've seen even  just in the most recent World Cup seeing  France kind of failed to even get it a  second time in a row so there are a  number of achievements here that I think  are are truly things that we'll live on  yeah and all the skills you know that we  see from our favorite players today  whether it's Neymar Messi Ronaldo Pele  did first didn't he he could create  emotions through football unlike any  other place  yeah I mean his his playstyle I think is  the thing that he's most known for the  fact that he kind of revolutionized the  game given the way that he approached it  with uh the kind of improvisation The  Joy  um you know the the kind of you know  breaking boundaries in a way going  outside the normal kind of rules or  um you know the kind of structures in  which the game had been played obviously  it was very much a kind of a European uh  invention a game that was dominated very  much by European side that all of a  sudden kind of Brazil emerges as this  Major Force largely because teams simply  had no answer for the style of play  um that the Brazilian team brought but  really personified in Pele as kind of  being the the player who kind of  approached it with such uh such Joy  spontaneity improvisation and we see it  in all of the clips that have since  circulated and the ones that of course  the more that that Global audiences then  had access to be able to see him not  just through television but eventually  through the kind of Tours that Santos  took to be able to travel to different  parts of the world yeah he did travel to  different parts to the world to Africa  and so on and and he United Abdullah he  United Brazil a country that's been torn  apart as we've seen recently by politics  uh he encapsulated really the powerful  Global unifying Spirit of football will  they ever be another football player  that did what he did  it's it's hard to to imagine that there  could be only because I think the  circumstances the context in which he  emerged is so so different now right now  we live in a much more uh you know media  dominated environment in which people  are basically their brands I think we  even saw that happen with parlay toward  the end of his life in which they become  much more commodified and so it's much  more difficult to kind of Imagine The  emergence of kind of you know figures  that that emerge in this kind of  spontaneous space where they can really  just personify the spirit of what they  embody on the field given the kinds of  adversity that he overcame when we know  about the background in terms of the  impoverishment that so many segments of  Brazilian Society encountered and then  of course the racism the anti-black  racism that existed and the lead up to  the 1958 World Cup that that kind of  allowed him to surpass many of those  kinds of challenges both within Brazil  and then of course globally you talked  about there about the evolution of  football what did he make of it what you  know the fact that football has become  so commercialized the business side of  it making it today one of the most  powerful Industries in the world what  did he make of that  well I mean I think if we look at his  later statements and and his kind of  encounters with some of today's uh  modern Superstars I mean there seemed to  be a kind of embracing of the power that  football brings the idea that it has to  capture imagination the idea that these  Superstars have a platform far bigger  even than the one that he may have had  during his career and so he was much  more welcoming and embracing of stars  like Neymar and Messi and others who of  course have a much more broader reach in  a global reach in the way that they are  without necessarily having the kind of  criticism perhaps of the commodification  of the sport in a way but hoping that at  least that this platform could be used  to kind of encourage  um you know a certain kind of coming  together that we see in things like the  World Cup most recently and other kinds  of events Abdullah thank you so much for  talking to us about this about pele's  Legacy El Abdullah Aryan joining us  there from Doha
 

